### run 1
=== native 64-bit kernels, or narrowing? ===
640x480, 15 interleaved rounds, minimum reported
binCV SIMD: NEON=yes AVX2=n/a popcount=hardware (fast paths active)

edgeThreshold, 8-bit in, 1-bit out
 (A) native uint32 buffer    257314 ns 1.00x
 (B) uint64 buffer, scalar fallback   2164487 ns  0.12x
 (C) uint64 buffer, narrowed view    257443 ns  1.00x

 (A) vs (C), pixel by pixel: 0 of 307200 differ

 memory (row-stride rounding, the reason chose 32):
 level 0       640x480  uint32   38400 B uint64   38400 B +0.0%
 level 1       320x240  uint32    9600 B uint64    9600 B +0.0%
 level 2       160x120  uint32    2400 B uint64    2880 B +20.0%
 level 3        80x60   uint32     720 B uint64     960 B +33.3%
 the case       94x60   uint32     720 B uint64     960 B +33.3%

If (C) reaches (A), a native 64-bit kernel can at best MATCH it --
same kernel, same bytes -- so there is nothing left for it to win, and
the answer is guidance rather than a second implementation of every
gated kernel.
